const GL=`Stock|A small slice of ownership in a company.
Share|One unit of a company's stock.
ETF|A fund you can buy like a stock that holds many investments.
Mutual fund|A pooled fund run by a manager, priced once a day.
Bond|A loan you give to a government or company that pays interest.
Yield|The income an investment pays each year, as a percent of its price.
Dividend|Cash a company pays to its shareholders.
Market cap|Total value of a company: share price times number of shares.
P/E ratio|Share price divided by yearly earnings per share.
EPS|Earnings per share: profit divided by number of shares.
Revenue|All the money a company brings in from sales.
Net income|Profit left after all costs, interest and taxes.
Margin|Profit as a percent of revenue.
Volatility|How much a price moves up and down.
Beta|How much a stock tends to move compared with the overall market.
Diversification|Spreading money across many investments to reduce risk.
Bull market|A period when prices are rising.
Bear market|A period when prices have fallen a lot, often 20% or more.
Inflation|The rate prices rise, which lowers what money can buy.
Interest rate|The cost of borrowing money, or the reward for saving.
Liquidity|How easily you can buy or sell without moving the price.
Volume|How many shares traded in a period.
Limit order|An order to buy or sell only at your chosen price or better.
Market order|An order to buy or sell right now at the current price.
Leverage|Using borrowed money to make bigger bets.
Drawdown|The drop from a peak to a low point.
Sharpe ratio|Return earned for each unit of risk taken.
Compound interest|Earning returns on your earlier returns.
IPO|A company's first sale of shares to the public.
Short selling|Betting a price will fall by selling borrowed shares.
Option|A contract giving the right to buy or sell at a set price.
DCF|Valuing a company by adding up its expected future cash, in today's money.`.split('\n').map(l=>l.split('|'));
